# Feedback

Every wait, outcome and absence gets exactly one feedback treatment.
The choice is mechanical — pick by situation, not by taste.

## Loading

| Situation | Use |
| --------- | --- |
| A page region is loading and its layout is known | `skeleton` shaped like the content |
| A button's action is in flight | the button's `loading` param (inline spinner, disabled) |
| An inline wait with no known shape or duration | `spinner` |
| A multi-step operation with measurable progress | `progress` |

Rules:

- Skeletons mirror the real layout (same card, same rows) so nothing
  jumps when content lands. Never show a skeleton for content that
  might be empty — resolve to the empty state directly.
- One loading signal at a time: a loading button does not also get a
  page spinner; a skeleton region does not also get a progress bar.
- `progress` only when progress is real. A bar that crawls to 90% and
  waits is worse than a spinner.

## Toast or alert

| | Toast | Alert |
| - | ----- | ----- |
| Answers | "Did my action work?" | "What state is this page in?" |
| Lifetime | Auto-dismisses (3 s; errors 5 s) | Stays until the state resolves |
| Placement | Overlay corner, via flash | In the page flow, where it applies |
| Examples | "Referral saved", "Merge failed" | "Results are more than 24 hours old" |

The rule: outcome of *the user's own action* → toast (rendered from
Rails flash by the `toast` partial). Ongoing *state of the data or
system* → alert, coloured by severity token. Anything the user must
not miss — critical clinical states above all — is an alert; a toast
can expire unseen. Confirmation of an action that needs a decision is
neither: that is a dialog.

## Empty states

Absence is content, not an error: plain body text following the
empty-state copy rules on the *Content* foundations page — what would
be here, how it gets here, with the action offered when one exists.
No alert styling, no state colour, no skeleton standing in for
nothing. Distinguish truly empty from filtered-to-nothing so the user
knows whether to add data or widen the filter.
